pipeline.md.template — the contract for an spp DECOMPOSITION pipeline
(DESIGN.md §7.1.12, the v0.11 arc).

A pipeline runs a LINEAR CHAIN of nodes — node 1 -> 2 -> ... -> terminal —
where each node is its own spp task (its own plan.md, baseline, optimization
loop, and REPORT) living under sub-tasks/<node-id>/. This file is the PARENT
contract: it declares the node ORDER and the inter-node WIRING (which
upstream output feeds which downstream input). Each node's OUTPUT_SCHEMA,
metric, and floor live in that node's own plan.md — the per-node contract,
unchanged from a single-node task. The per-stage isolation contract applies
per node exactly as it does for a single-node task (DESIGN.md §7.1.12):
optimizing a node sees only that node's local input -> output -> gold.

A single-node task needs NO pipeline.md — that is the default, and it runs
exactly as before. pipeline.md exists only when the user adopts a
decomposition (the structure-advisor `decomposition` entry). This is the
MANAGED form of the manual feature-group-splitting practice (DESIGN.md §10
glossary); the manual practice — independent sub-tasks the user coordinates
by hand — stays valid and coexists.

Produced by:  the designer during /spp-init, when the user adopts a
              decomposition. The designer also runs /spp-init for each node.
Consumed by:  /spp-baseline (materializes each node's baseline in dependency
              order from the frozen upstream output), /spp-loop (optimizes the
              active node upstream-frozen), /spp-finalize (composite scoring on
              the sacred test set, read once across the whole pipeline). Each
              command re-reads this file fresh and verifies its actions are
              still on-spec (the plan.md-as-contract rule, DESIGN.md §7.1.1
              invariant #15, applied at the pipeline level).
Mid-pipeline changes:  append a dated entry to the revision log (§5) with the
              reason. Do not silently rewrite earlier sections.

Placeholder syntax: {{PLACEHOLDER_NAME}}. Each placeholder has an inline HTML
comment describing what fills it, by whom, at what stage. The Phase 4 template
linter (tests/) will verify all placeholders are resolved and the validation
rules below hold before the file is treated as a final pipeline spec.
-->

